# Trivy vulnerability ignore list — kept in sync with composer.json audit.ignored
#
# Each entry: CVE-XXXX-XXXXX with justification comment above.
# Trivy reads this file from the working directory by default.

# Symfony http-foundation CVE-2025-64500 — fix only available in Symfony 7.3.7+,
# requires full Symfony 7.2 → 7.3 migration (planned, separate spec).
# Project is currently pinned to Symfony 7.2.* in composer.json.
CVE-2025-64500

# ─── OS-level CVEs from the Debian base of php:8.3.27-cli ────────────────────
# These are reported by Trivy when scanning our image but are NOT exploitable
# in our runtime profile. They will clear automatically when we rebase onto a
# newer php:8.x base image (tracked separately).

# Linux kernel CVEs — containers share the HOST kernel, so kernel CVEs in the
# container image are red herrings: patching them requires updating the host
# (Railway/CI runner), not our application image. The kernel package is only
# present in the base image as a metadata leftover and is never executed.
CVE-2026-43469
CVE-2026-43472
CVE-2026-43473
# Additional kernel CVEs disclosed June 2026 — same rationale, kernel never
# executed inside the container, fix requires host kernel update.
CVE-2026-46241
CVE-2026-46243
CVE-2026-46246
CVE-2026-46267
CVE-2026-46300
CVE-2026-46333

# ncurses-base / ncurses-bin buffer overflow — ncurses is only invoked by
# interactive shells / TUIs, none of which our PHP-FPM/CLI workload triggers.
# No reachable code path from our application or its direct dependencies.
CVE-2025-69720
